<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> Autonomous Oblast was an autonomous region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> SSR, created in 1923 for the mountainous part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, which had an Armenian majority. Its demand in 1988 for transfer to Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> began the Karabakh conflict. Azerbaijan abolished the oblast in November 1991, after the region had proclaimed itself the Nagorno-Karabakh Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh-autonomous-oblast.jpg" alt="Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagorno-Karabakh_AO_in_the_Soviet_Union_(European_part).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> was disputed between the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic during their short-lived independence from 1918 to 1920. After both became Soviet, the Kavbiuro of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Bolshevik Party</a> decided to keep the area within the new Azerbaijan SSR while granting it broad regional autonomy.</p><p>At first the principal city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>, and its surrounding villages were to be left out of the autonomy, since they were predominantly Azerbaijani, particularly after the massacre and expulsion of the majority Armenian population of Shusha. That decision was reversed in 1923, when Shusha was joined to the oblast despite protests from Muslim villages that favoured inclusion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> uezd instead. The oblast was formally established on 7 July 1923, and its capital was moved to Stepanakert, named after the revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepan-shaumian">Stepan Shaumian</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>At its formation the oblast covered 4,161 square kilometres. In the 1926 Soviet census its population was 125,200, of whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were 89.2 percent. By the 1989 census the Armenian share had fallen to 76.9 percent. The reasons given include a policy of Soviet Azerbaijani authorities to settle Azerbaijanis in the region, some out-migration of Karabakh Armenians, and a generally higher birth rate among Azerbaijanis than among Armenians.</p><p>The oblast had five districts: Mardakert, Martuni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>, Askeran and Hadrut. Its highest executive power lay with the First Secretary of the oblast committee of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, a post created in July 1923 and abolished on 27 August 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The status of the oblast became a major public issue only in the late 1980s, but Karabakh Armenian activists, intellectuals and Soviet Armenian leaders had appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> for its transfer to the Armenian SSR before. In November 1945 the Armenian First Secretary Grigory Arutinov appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, a proposal vetoed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>'s Mir Jafar Baghirov. In 1966 an appeal signed by 1,906 Armenian intellectual and cultural figures was likewise vetoed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>.</p><p>Inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>'s glasnost and perestroika, Karabakh Armenians began a mass movement to unite the region with Soviet Armenia. On 20 February 1988 the Supreme Soviet of the oblast voted to unify with Soviet Armenia. Open conflict followed between the local Armenian population and the government of the Azerbaijan SSR, and it escalated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a> by the end of 1991. On 26 November 1991 the Supreme Soviet of Azerbaijan abolished the oblast's autonomous status, and on 10 December the majority Armenian population declared independence as the Nagorno-Karabakh Republic, with the support of Armenia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Gorbachev's glasnost and perestroika the Supreme Soviet of the <b>Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast</b> voted on 20 February 1988 to unite with Armenia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic included the <b>Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast</b> and the Nakhchivan ASSR.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-ssr">Azerbaijan SSR</a></li></ul></section>
